{"qid":"Q172579","names":["Kingdom of Italy"],"title":"Kingdom of Italy","endonym":{"native":"Regno d'Italia","lang":"it"},"capsule":"The dataset's span of 587–1946 for the 'Kingdom of Italy' (Regno d'Italia) telescopes two distinct realms sharing the name and the Italian peninsula. The medieval Regnum Italiae arose from the Lombard kingdom of northern Italy and persisted as a constituent crown of the Frankish and then Holy Roman emperors. The modern Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed on 17 March 1861 when Vittorio Emanuele II of Savoy was crowned king after the Risorgimento, with Rome incorporated as capital in 1871; Mussolini's Fascist dictatorship (1922–1943) drew Italy into the Axis and military catastrophe, and a referendum on 2 June 1946 abolished the monarchy and established the Italian Republic.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Post-1861 Italian territorial extent is precisely documented in international treaties; the dataset's early start date of 587 reflects a geometric entry encompassing the Lombard kingdom period, which is historically attested but geographically less precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":"topic/Kingdom-of-Italy","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Italy","rulers":[{"qid":"Q166092","name":"Camillo Benso di Cavour","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1861,"end":1861,"image":"11 Meyer-Pierson.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/11%20Meyer-Pierson.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Léopold-Ernest Mayer, Pierre-Louis Pierson","attributionRequired":false}},{"qid":null,"name":"Bettino Ricasoli (1